    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change follows this codebase's established structure exactly.\n\n- **`getSalutation()` is in the right unit.** `Owner` already hosts a whole family of pure, response-time derived getters \u2014 `getAgeBand()`, `getMembershipPoints()`, `getMembershipLevel()`, `getLocality()`, `getTimezone()`, `getContactPreference()`, `getCheckDigit()`, `getIdentityKey()`, `getTelephoneDisplay()`. Each is a pure function of already-present fields, documented with the same \"derived at response time \u2026 Not persisted\" javadoc convention. `getSalutation()` matches that idiom precisely (pure function of `title` + `lastName`), and its javadoc is consistent with its siblings. It correctly does *not* use the `DeriveOwner<X>` function-class + transient-field pattern, which is reserved for derivations that need the repository (`DeriveOwnerHouseholdSize`, `DeriveOwnerBulkSignupWarning`) \u2014 salutation needs no external data, so a computed getter is the natural fit and MapStruct picks it up by property name automatically.\n\n- **`title` is a normal persisted field**, wired in the standard places: `@Column` + getter/setter adjacent to the other simple fields, and copied in `ApplyOwner.service` alongside the other `owner.setX(request.getX())` lines \u2014 the right unit for request\u2192entity field application.\n\n- **Schema and OpenAPI edits are consistent and additive**: `title VARCHAR(3)`/`TEXT` added in the same position across all four dialects; OpenAPI adds `title` (request, validated pattern) and `salutation` (response, `readOnly`) with descriptions matching the entity javadoc.\n\nNothing is duplicated, no class or method is becoming a catch-all, and a maintainer of this repo would find each piece exactly where they'd expect it.",
